! jb_ax5b "I-Box 5", routine 1262 ("TEST EX AND EXIC"), TEST 1B --
! "-TEST 1B, CHK EX OF EACH TYPE OF / -INSTRUCTION, DIR IX." -- entry
! label 16212, PDF pp.90-91, octal 023373.40-023442.00.  76 rows.
!
! THIS IS THE ACCEPTANCE TEST FOR **EX**, and EX is the first thing in
! it that no file in this repo has ever witnessed.  Its twin TEST 2B
! (programs/ax5b_162_exic_classes.strap) brought in EXIC; every other
! mnemonic on these two pages -- SR, BZXCZ, BRZ, L(BU), BZXE, BZXVZ,
! SVA, KV, LV, V+ -- is already witnessed elsewhere in strap/verify_*.
! EX is the one new bit pattern, and it appears five times.  Checked
! rather than assumed: no strap/*.py and no other programs/*.strap
! holds a bare "EX," operand.  The only other mention anywhere is
! ax5b_1681_if_close_to_ds.strap's header line, "1B and 1C need EX,
! deferred in docs/NEXT.md section 5" -- the gap this file closes.
!
! CITED BY PDF PAGE, and that is not a slip.  jb_ax5b's LISTING pages
! carry no printed page numbers at all -- the "12 11 10 9" down the
! left margin is fanfold carriage-tape line numbering, and the only
! printed numbers are in the front write-up, which is scanned 2-up.
! Every verify_ax5b_*.py here already says "PDF p.NN" for that reason.
!
! WHAT TEST 1B DOES.  It sweeps the same five instruction classes TEST
! 2B sweeps -- DIR IX, IMMED IX, MISC, FLT PT, FULL WD VFL -- in the
! same order, and for each one asks TWO questions, not three:
!
!   (a) did the subject instruction TAKE EFFECT?
!   (b) did EX leave the SUBJECT WORD intact?
!
! and (b) is checked by comparing the slot 162EXT against the master
! copy the class was loaded from -- LV/KV in the DIR IX block, KV alone
! in the other four.
!
! WHY ONLY TWO CHECKS, when 2B has three.  2B's extra rule is "EXIC to
! this class must not interrupt", and it works by planting a handler
! address into the interrupt-table entry 162ITE and reaching it only on
! an interruption.  1B plants an address into 162ITE too -- but look at
! WHICH address.  In every one of the five classes the planted label is
! the row IMMEDIATELY AFTER the EX:
!
!     EX at 023377.00  ->  16213 at 023377.40
!     EX at 023406.40  ->  16214 at 023407.00
!     EX at 023415.00  ->  16215 at 023415.40
!     EX at 023423.40  ->  16216 at 023424.00
!     EX at 023432.40  ->  16217 at 023433.00
!
! An interruption and an ordinary fall-through therefore land in the
! same place, so there is nothing for a separate "-INT WHEN EX TO"
! message to say.  That is the structural difference between 1B and 2B,
! and it is measured off the bit-18 decodes below, not assumed.
!
! THE REPORTED ERROR TEXT IS INCONSISTENT AND THAT IS PRESERVED.  Read
! first-hand off the comment column at 600 dpi, exactly as printed:
!
!   DIR IX    "-UNABLE TO EX DIR IX INST."      "-EX DESTROYED SUBJ INST."
!   IMMED IX  "-UNABLE TO EX IMMED IX INSTR."   "-EX DESTROYED SUBJ INST."
!   MISC      "-UNABLE TO EX MISC INSTR."       "-EX DESTROYED SUBJ INSTR."
!   FLT PT    "-UNABLE TO EX FP INST."          "-EX DESTROYED SUBJ INST."
!   FULL WD   "-UNABLE TO EX FULL WD VFL INST." "-EX DESTROYS SUBJ INST."
!
! "INST." against "INSTR.", and "DESTROYED" against "DESTROYS", within
! one block of one routine.  Not normalised.  The class headers vary
! the same way: "-IMMED IX.", "-MISC." and "-FULL WD." carry a period
! and "-FLT PT" does not; "-TO SSIP" here has no period where the same
! row on PDF p.110 prints "-TO SSIP.".
!
! BZXVZ PLAYS TWO OPPOSITE ROLES HERE and must not be tidied.  In DIR
! IX and IMMED IX it is a SKIP: "BZXVZ,$+2.0 / BZXCZ,$+1.32" both land
! on the check-(b) row (023402.00 and 023411.40 respectively, decoded
! below), jumping OVER the two-row error entry when the index value or
! the index count came out non-zero.  In MISC it is the jump TO the
! error, "BZXVZ,SERS".  The polarity is the exic twin's argument,
! transferred: MISC does no "Z,$X1" of its own and its subject
! (162EXM = "Z,$X1", PDF p.109) is expected to ZERO the value, so for
! MISC a non-zero value is the failure; the other classes zero $X1
! first and expect the subject to make it non-zero.
!
! THE Z,$R / Z,$L ORDER INVERTS BETWEEN THE TWO ACCUMULATOR CLASSES and
! that is what the page prints.  FLT PT clears $R then $L (023422.40 =
! "11.22 00", 023423.00 = "10.22 00"); FULL WD VFL clears $L then $R
! (023431.40 = "10.22 00", 023432.00 = "11.22 00").  The encodings pass
! either way round, which is exactly why the order is called out: a
! tidy-up here would silently stop being bit-exact to the page.
!
! THE DIR IX BLOCK CHECKS (b) DIFFERENTLY FROM THE OTHER FOUR, and this
! file does not explain why.  DIR IX does "LV,$X1,162EXT / KV,$X1,
! 162EX" -- pull the value out of the slot into index 1, then compare
! index 1 against the master.  The other four do "KV,$X2,162EXT" alone,
! comparing the still-loaded $X2 straight against the slot.  Stated
! rather than rationalised, in the manner of ax5b_16082_op_ad.strap's
! "NOT TRACED" block; the ENCODING of both shapes is settled either
! way, and that is what is checked.
!
! NO CNOP IN THIS RANGE, and the absence is deliberate.  Both full-word
! L(BU) rows fall on word boundaries by ordinary flow -- 023424.00 and
! 023433.00 -- so the deck needed no alignment pseudo-op here.  PDF
! p.109's constants area is full of CNOPs; these pages have none.  Do
! not add one.
!
! THE SUBJECT INSTRUCTIONS ARE DEFINED ON PDF pp.109-110, read here at
! 600 dpi, and their label column confirms eight of this file's SYNs:
!     162ITE  BD,$             -ALL INTERRUPTS ENTER HERE.   024320.00
!     162EXT  XW,0,0,0                                       024321.00
!     162EX   LX,$X1,100O      -KEEP -IN -ORDER              024322.00
!     162XW1  XW,0,64,162INT                                 024326.00
!     162EXV  L%BU@,100O       -VFL FULL WD.                 024327.00
!     162EXF  L%U@,100O        -FLT PT.                      024330.00
!     162EXM  Z,$X1            -MISC.                        024331.00
!     162EXI  LVI,$X1,%8@777777.40  -IMMED IX.  (p.110)      024332.00
! The operand "100O" is a symbol, not the number one thousand: it is
! the constant-pool name ax5b_160_test8b.strap's header already places
! at 024503.00, which is the address 162EX/162EXV/162EXF actually
! print.  162EXF's "L%U@" is the floating-point spelling and 162EXV's
! "L%BU@" the VFL one, matching the two "L%BU@" rows transcribed below.
!
! ENCODING ONLY, and it could not be otherwise: a run needs the 64-word
! interrupt table, SSIP's SEN/SERS/SSW reporting, the whole 162* con-
! stants area -- and an EX the model actually EXECUTES.  EX is in
! MISC_CODES, so the ASSEMBLER encodes it; that is the whole
! distinction, and it is the distinction this file exists to close.
!
! SUBSTITUTIONS: numeric labels take an "L" prefix (L16212 ... L16217,
! L162ITE and friends), as ax5b_162_exic_classes.strap already does for
! 1262.  "L%BU@" is written "L(BU)", the spelling
! programs/ax5b_160_test8b.strap uses for the identical row shape.
!
! ONE GLYPH CAVEAT THAT CHANGES NO BIT.  The fourth character of SEN0
! reads as a letter O on this printer, here and on PDF p.110, so the
! symbol may really be "SENO"; ax5b_160_test8b.strap measured it at 54
! px on PDF p.114 and kept the digit spelling anyway.  Kept as SEN0 for
! the same reason -- it is a SYN taken from the row that names it.
!
! EXTERNALS, SYN'd at the addresses this same listing prints.  THESE
! ARE INPUTS TO THE CHECK, NOT WITNESSES -- the same caveat
! verify_ax5b_16082_16083_listing.py makes about L16088.  A SYN row
! MAKES the address field come out right, so a row naming one proves
! only that every OTHER field lands where the listing puts it.  See the
! verify file for which of them have a second, independent source and
! which (IC262) have none.
!
! Transcription standard: bit-exact against the printed machine code,
! verify_ax5b_162_test1b_listing.py.

        SLC, (8)23373.40
!
! ---- DIR IX: set the trap, plant the subject, EX it ---------------
L16212  Z,   $X1                   ! -TEST 1B, CHK EX OF EACH TYPE OF
        LVI, $X2, L16213           ! -INSTRUCTION, DIR IX.
        SVA, $X2, L162ITE          ! the "handler" is the row after the EX
        LX,  $X2, L162XW1          ! the index word whose refill is 162INT
        SR,  $X2, $IA              ! point the Interruption Address there
        LX,  $X2, L162EX           ! the DIR IX subject instruction
        SX,  $X2, L162EXT          ! into the subject slot
        EX,  L162EXT
L16213  LX,  $X1, $X1              ! (a) did the subject take effect?
        BZXVZ, $+2.0               !   value non-zero -> skip the error
        BZXCZ, $+1.32              !   count non-zero -> skip the error
        SIC, SEN
        B,   SERS                  ! -UNABLE TO EX DIR IX INST.
        LV,  $X1, L162EXT          ! (b) is the subject word still intact?
        KV,  $X1, L162EX           !   compare against the master copy
        SIC, SEN
        BZXE, SERS                 ! -EX DESTROYED SUBJ INST.
!
! ---- IMMED IX ----------------------------------------------------
        Z,   $X1                   ! -IMMED IX.
        LVI, $X2, L16214
        SVA, $X2, L162ITE
        LX,  $X2, L162EXI          ! "LVI,$X1,%8@777777.40", PDF p.110
        SX,  $X2, L162EXT
        EX,  L162EXT
L16214  LX,  $X1, $X1
        BZXVZ, $+2.0
        BZXCZ, $+1.32
        SIC, SEN
        B,   SERS                  ! -UNABLE TO EX IMMED IX INSTR.
        KV,  $X2, L162EXT          ! $X2 still holds the master copy
        SIC, SEN
        BZXE, SERS                 ! -EX DESTROYED SUBJ INST.
!
! ---- MISC: no Z,$X1 first, so the polarity flips -----------------
        LVI, $X1, L16215           ! -MISC.
        SVA, $X1, L162ITE
        LX,  $X2, L162EXM          ! the subject is itself "Z,$X1"
        SX,  $X2, L162EXT
        EX,  L162EXT
L16215  LX,  $X1, $X1
        SIC, SEN
        BZXVZ, SERS                ! -UNABLE TO EX MISC INSTR.
        KV,  $X2, L162EXT
        SIC, SEN
        BZXE, SERS                 ! -EX DESTROYED SUBJ INSTR.
!
! ---- FLT PT: clears $R then $L, and reads $L back ----------------
        LVI, $X1, L16216           ! -FLT PT   (no period on the page)
        SVA, $X1, L162ITE
        LX,  $X2, L162EXF          ! "L%U@,100O", PDF p.109
        SX,  $X2, L162EXT
        Z,   $R                    ! $R first here -- see the header
        Z,   $L
        EX,  L162EXT
L16216  L(BU), $L                  ! FULL WORD: read $L back, 64 bits BU
        SIC, SEN
        BRZ, SERS                  ! -UNABLE TO EX FP INST.
        KV,  $X2, L162EXT
        SIC, SEN
        BZXE, SERS                 ! -EX DESTROYED SUBJ INST.
!
! ---- FULL WD VFL: clears $L then $R, and reads $R back -----------
        LVI, $X1, L16217           ! -FULL WD.
        SVA, $X1, L162ITE
        LX,  $X2, L162EXV          ! "L%BU@,100O", PDF p.109
        SX,  $X2, L162EXT
        Z,   $L                    ! $L first here -- the inverse order
        Z,   $R
        EX,  L162EXT
L16217  L(BU), $R                  ! FULL WORD: read $R back, 64 bits BU
        SIC, SEN
        BRZ, SERS                  ! -UNABLE TO EX FULL WD VFL INST.
        KV,  $X2, L162EXT
        SIC, SEN
        BZXE, SERS                 ! -EX DESTROYS SUBJ INST.
!
! ---- the exit: back to 16212 once, then out to SSIP --------------
        B,   $+1.0
        BD,  L16212
        SIC, SEN0+.32
        B,   SSW                   ! -TO SSIP   (no period on the page)
        BD,  $+.32
!
! ---- the routine's continuity word -------------------------------
        LX,  $X13, IC262           ! -UPDATE CONTINUITY CHECK.
        V+,  $X13, BIT1
        SX,  $X13, IC262
!
! ---- externals, SYN'd at the addresses this same listing prints --
!   INPUTS, NOT WITNESSES.  Eight are confirmed first-hand by the label
!   column of PDF pp.109-110 (see the header); SEN/SERS/SEN0/SSW/BIT1
!   rest on other verify files in this repo; IC262 rests on nothing but
!   the operand field on these two pages.
SEN     SYN, (8)1310.0             ! the SIC error-entry link
SERS    SYN, (8)1304.0             ! the error-report routine
SEN0    SYN, (8)1311.0             ! may really be "SENO" -- see below
SSW     SYN, (8)1301.0             ! the SSIP return
L162ITE SYN, (8)24320.0            ! the interrupt-table entry, p.109
L162EXT SYN, (8)24321.0            ! the subject-instruction slot, p.109
L162EX  SYN, (8)24322.0            ! the DIR IX subject, p.109
L162XW1 SYN, (8)24326.0            ! the index word for $IA, p.109
L162EXV SYN, (8)24327.0            ! the VFL full-word subject, p.109
L162EXF SYN, (8)24330.0            ! the floating-point subject, p.109
L162EXM SYN, (8)24331.0            ! the MISC subject, p.109
L162EXI SYN, (8)24332.0            ! the IMMED IX subject, PDF p.110
IC262   SYN, (8)24171.0            ! 1262's continuity word
BIT1    SYN, (8)24523.0            ! 024522 + 1
        END, L16212
